# Airlock tracks the MECHANISM. Anything a live run writes, and anything
# that describes one particular machine, stays out of the repo.
#
# The directories under agent/ are tracked but their CONTENTS are not:
# each carries its own .gitignore holding `*` and `!.gitignore`, so a
# fresh clone has drop/ out/ logs/ status/ present and empty and the
# daemon has somewhere to write on the first run. Those four files are
# the "fresh clone works" structure — do not delete them.

# ---- runtime state, written by a run -------------------------------------
agent/drop/*
!agent/drop/.gitignore
agent/out/*
!agent/out/.gitignore
agent/logs/*
!agent/logs/.gitignore
agent/status/*
!agent/status/.gitignore
agent/batch.json
agent/.batch.json.tmp.*
agent/batch.json.airlock.tmp

# ---- per-machine configuration -------------------------------------------
# Which host directories THIS install exposes. mounts.conf.example is
# committed and is the documentation; the real file never is.
mounts.conf

# Which hosts THIS install may reach. Same pattern: the .example is the
# committed baseline a new install copies from.
proxy/allowlist.txt

# ---- captured records of one machine's runs ------------------------------
# probe_host.sh, report.sh, submit_project.sh and install_quadlet.sh all
# write into DevComms/. Those files name real paths on a real machine and
# are records, not documentation. The hand-written logs are tracked.
DevComms/host_inventory.txt
DevComms/sandbox_report.txt
DevComms/last_run.txt
DevComms/quadlet_install.txt

# ---- one person's host workflow ------------------------------------------
# A script that names one machine's layout and drives that machine's GitHub
# account is exactly what SandboxDesign's create_github_repo.sh was, and why
# it was not carried across. This one is deliberately gitignored; it lives on
# the machine that runs it and is never published.
create_public_repo.sh

# Same category: the one-shot force-push of the rewritten history. It drives
# one account's remote and is a local operation, never a published one.
force_push_scrubbed.sh

# Same category again: the routine add-commit-push driver and its
# read-only pre-flight. Both hardcode this repo's install location the
# same way create_public_repo.sh does, and both drive this machine's git
# identity and remote. See DevComms/log_002_pii_scrub.md, 2026-08-22
# tidiness-pass section, for why this pair exists and what it refuses.
git_commit_push.sh
git_status.sh

# The message handoff git_commit_push.sh reads from and empties after
# each use. It is written by whichever session prepares a commit and is
# never meaningful outside that one run.
DevComms/next_commit_message.txt

# ---- one-off migration handoff -------------------------------------------
# Replacement text for the toolchain skill's SandboxDesign sections, and any
# other skill handoff dropped in DevComms/. These name one machine's skill
# cache, its hardware and its project repos, so tracking one would be the
# project-specific drift README.md's contract forbids - and would publish
# personal information. They are handoff documents for a session, not
# documentation of the tool. Delete them once the skill is saved.
#
# The glob is deliberate: an earlier handoff sat here under a name the
# single-file rule did not cover, untracked but NOT ignored, one `git add -A`
# away from publication. scrub_check.sh now flags that class as well.
DevComms/toolchain_skill_airlock_patch.md
DevComms/toolchain_*
DevComms/*_SKILL_*

# ---- local-only scrub patterns -------------------------------------------
# Extra "<label>|<regex>" lines for scrub_check.sh that are themselves too
# specific to a person or machine to publish.
.scrub_patterns.local

# ---- build and editor artefacts ------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/
*.log
run_logs/
